      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=2 x + 3 y^{2}$ at the point $(1,2)$ in the direction of the vector $v=2\\,i-1\\,j.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(2\\,i-1\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x + 3 y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x + 3 y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,6 y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,2) & =⟨2,6 (2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,2) & =∇f(1,2)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨2,12⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5},- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)\\cdot (2)+(12)\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{8 \\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
